{
  "image_name": "2b1df475a3357fc05b17c897ac8572e0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how defective efferocytosis—the clearance of apoptotic cells—contributes to chronic inflammatory disease in lupus/EAE, diabetes or obesity, and atherosclerosis. Across all three conditions, the central theme is that phosphatidylserine (PS) recognition on dying cells and PS-receptor function are impaired, so dead cells are not efficiently removed and inflammation persists.\n\nOn the left, lupus/EAE is linked to reduced exposure of PS and cleavage of PS receptors such as Axl, MerTK, and Tyro3 by ADAM17/10, along with impaired LXR/PPARγ signaling and SCARF1-related defects. The middle section shows diabetes/obesity, where high glucose and obesity-associated inflammation appear to alter receptor pathways through ADAM9, reduced miR-126, disrupted erythropoietin and S1P signaling, and lower PPARγ/δ activity. On the right, atherosclerosis features high oxidized lipids and inflammatory cytokines, increased TNF, cleavage of receptors, degradation of LRP1, reduced ABCA1-mediated cholesterol export, and higher CD47 on foam cells, all of which block uptake and resolution.\n\nThe bottom portion highlights matching therapeutic strategies, including antibodies against soluble receptor cleavage products, PPARγ/δ agonists, anti-CD47 approaches, nucleic acid or protein replacement therapies, and nanoparticle-based delivery systems to restore pro-resolving clearance.",
